 hand is separate. So after forming the Chi W3, my hand would have the new meld [W2, W3, W4], and the existing [B5, B6, B7]. Then the remaining tiles would be W4 (but wait, W4 is already in the hand. Wait, the Chi uses W2, W3 (from discard), and W4 from my hand? Or do I have W2 and W4 in my hand, and take W3 to form the Chow. So the Chow would be W2, W3, W4. That's correct.\\n\\nSo after forming the Chow, my hand would have the melds [[B5, B6, B7], [W2, W3, W4]], and the remaining tiles: W4 (but wait, I had one W4 in my hand. If I used it in the Chow, then I don't have it anymore. Wait no, the Chow requires three tiles. I have W2 and W4 in my hand. The discarded W3 is taken, so the Chow is W2 (my tile), W3 (discarded), and W4 (my tile). So the W4 is part of the Chow. So after forming the Chow, my hand would have the remaining tiles: T1, T3, T4, T9, B2, B4, F2. Plus the melds. Let me count. Original hand had 10 tiles (since melds take 3, and total is 13). After forming a Chi, the hand reduces by two tiles (since you use two from your hand and take one discarded). So 10 - 2 = 8 tiles left. Let's check: original hand after melds is 10. After Chi, the remaining tiles would be 10 - 2 = 8. So the remaining tiles are T1, T3, T4, T9, B2, B4, F2, and one more. Wait, maybe I missed a tile. Let me recount.\\n\\nOriginal hand tiles (excluding melds): W2, W4, T1, T3, T4, T9, B2, B4, B5, F2. That's 10 tiles. After forming the Chi [W2, W3, W4], the W2 and W4 are used up. So remaining tiles are: T1, T3, T4, T9, B2, B4, B5, F2. That's 8 tiles. Now, with the two melds, that's 3+3=6 tiles in melds. 8 remaining in hand. So total 14 tiles. Wait, no, the total should be 13. Wait, maybe I made a mistake. The initial state: hand has 10 tiles, melds have 3. Total 13. After Chi, the hand has 10 - 2 = 8, and melds have 3 + 3 = 6. 8 + 6 = 14? That can't be right. Wait, no. When you form a Chi, you take one tile from the discard, and use two from your hand. So total tiles in hand after Chi woul